{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n12 \n \n \nsupport the operations and provide reference images.  The 12-bit digital camera records at up to \n30 full frames (1360x1024) per second, and is equipped with a motorized lens with independent \ncontrol of the zoom, iris, and focus.  The digital camera will be equipped with a filter assembly \nwith four options, where selection of a clear \u201cfilter\u201d allows for color imaging.  Color imaging can \nalso be carried out with a blue-green BG7 Schott glass filter, which balances the color planes to \navoid saturation in the red while maintaining sensitivity in the blue and green.  The natural flame \ncolor can be approximately reconstructed, but the primary purpose of the filter is pyrometry and \nmeasurement of the soot volume fraction, again via the techniques of [23-24].  Alternately, CH* \nimaging can be conducted with this camera because the filter assembly includes a 430-nm \nbandpass filter with a 10-nm full width at half-maximum transmission (FWHM).  A 450-nm \nbandpass filter (again with a 10-nm FWHM) can be used with the CH* filter to effectively subtract \ninterfering broadband soot emission.  However, the filter assembly rotates slowly so such \nsubtraction effectively requires a separate test with each filter and of course reproducible flame \nconditions. \n \nThe video from the analog operations (i.e., ops) camera, also referred to as the internal camera, \nwas not archived in space.  The video was instead recorded on the ground in the operations center.  \nIts gain was sometimes adjusted for improved imaging of dim flames, e.g., approaching extinction.   \n \nThe sensitivity of the color digital camera, often referenced as the ACME DataCam (i.e., Data \nCamera), to the dim microgravity flames was such that it was often used with long exposure times \nand thus low framing rates, for example on the order of 1 frame per second.  But as an alternate, \nit was sometimes instead used with high gain values. \n \nThe clear \u201cfilter\u201d is of fused silica.  Unfortunately, the thickness of the blue-green BG7 Schott glass \nfilter and the added antireflective (AR) coating caused too great a reduction in the red, so it does \nnot balance the intensity of the three color planes as intended and thereby can limit the accuracy \nof temperature determination.  Meanwhile, rather than the 450-nm bandpass filter described \nabove, the high-definition color camera was equipped with a bandpass filter for imaging", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 12}}